SM(322,{"n":"Leviticus 1.1-5","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Leviticus 1.1-5.mp3","d":2329.5,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.0,"Okay. So we'll go ahead and get started."],[25.04,"And we are starting a new book."],[26.8,"We are starting the book of Leviticus, everybody's favorite book of the Bible."],[30.8,"Just fun times beginning to end."],[34.32,"So far at least, what happened the last time I went through the Old Testament has proven true."],[41.84,"I'm always a little worried."],[43.6,"Or both times I've been worried."],[45.44,"You you know know,"],[45.76,"I go through I go through Genesis and a bit well, there's a lot of stuff here."],[49.12,"People come to Genesis, and then you get into the first half of Exodus."],[53.2,"And I'm like, W,ell people who come for this, there's a lot of cool stuff happening coming out of Egypt and Mount Sinai and everything."],[59.72,"Then you get to sort of the second half of Exodus with all the measurements and the tabernacle and stuff, and then you start getting into Leviticus, and I worry, like, is everyone gonna just stop coming at this point?"],[71.24,"They find it tedious and boring, but you haven't, so congratulations, y'all."],[76.44,"But there is actually a lot of good stuff in the in the book of Leviticus."],[80.84,"So the introduction to Leviticus is not gonna be as long as the last couple introductions, because for example, we already had the introduction to the Torah."],[90.36,"This is part of the Torah, and Leviticus does not narrate a lot of history."],[100.12,"So when we got into the introduction to Exodus, we had lots of things about Egypt and about, you know, the time when this is taking place and background and these kind of things."],[110.48,"And there's really not that for Leviticus."],[112.88,"Leviticus, the first seven chapters are basically God telling Moses things to tell the people of Israel."],[127.36,"And then chapters 11 through 27, which is the end of the book, are God telling Moses things to tell to the people of Israel."],[137.28,"And then there's like these three chapters in the middle where we read about the ordination of the priests and the stuff that happens at that time."],[146.8,"But that's the only actual like narrative piece."],[153.13,"So there's not a huge span of time in terms of Israel's journey that's happening here."],[160.65,"And in fact, remember, we left off with the dedication of the tabernacle."],[166.09,"When we get to chapter 8, we get into those chapters, we're gonna have it's gonna pick up at the dedication of the tabernacle with the ordination of the priests."],[174.33,"So I mean, like, it's basically we're gonna move forward in time one episode, basically, like one like a few days over the course of the whole book of Leviticus."],[185.45,"So the background of the book of Leviticus is really the background of the end of the book of Exodus."],[191.15,"It's the same time and everything."],[193.63,"And then numbers is gonna pick up right on the other side of that"],[198.63,"We're not in a different time, we're not in a different place, we're out in the desert right after leaving Sinai, right at the dedication of the tabernacle."],[206.55,"So it's not a lot of that background to go over here by way of introduction."],[212.87,"So the the name of the book is equally boring."],[217.51,"Leviticus is the Latin basically transliteration of Leviticon in Greek, which means the Levite thing in Greek."],[231.11,"So thing here being book."],[233.11,"It's the book of the Levites."],[234.63,"This is the stuff for the Levites, for the priests."],[236.79,"It's the priest book."],[238.87,"Not super exciting."],[240.87,"But the Hebrew name is even more boring."],[244.71,"Because we've I think we've mentioned this with Genesis and Exodus."],[249.03,"The Hebrew names of the five books in the Torah are just the first word."],[257.51,"So Genesis is called Bareshit, which means in the beginning, because that's the first word."],[264.84,"So the name of Leviticus, the Hebrew name of Leviticus is Vyakra, which means, and he called out."],[273.8,"Because as you can see, if you look at if you look at one verse one, now the Lord called to Moses."],[280.44,"So Hebrew is written verb. subject, object normally"],[285.12,"So Viacra is the and he called to, and then the next word is Yahweh."],[291.65,"So Yahweh called to Moses, and then Moses with the direct object marker."],[296.45,"So yeah, that's not super exciting to break down."],[301.25,"Now I will note, because I'm trying to make it interesting, that tried real hard."],[307.01,"The the vi in vaihra is actually the marker for continuous narrative."],[315.57,"So since verbs begin most Hebrew sentences, that verb will have a marker in front of it to indicate either continuityity with with what came before or discontinu, like a break what came before."],[333.77,"So good English translations will indicate when there's that continuity marker, they'll have like then da-da-da-da-da, or so da-da-da-da-da, right to indicate that sort of continuous."],[349.13,"And if it has the discontinuity, they'll do something like but or afterward or later."],[357.39,"To try to, depending on the context, to try to indicate the separation."],[362.59,"So the fact that Leviticus begins with a continuity marker sort of emphasizes what I was saying about the background."],[372.57,"The first word of the book indicates continuity with the end of Exodus."],[378.46,"It's connecting it right back to pre spoke."],[381.82,"So Leviticus is a text that can't really function on its own."],[388.46,"Maybe if you stripped out chunks of it."],[391.6,"Like if you took out those three chapters about the ordination of the priest, like you took the narrative out and just kept God saying to Moses, tell the Israelites this."],[400.98,"You can turn it into some kind of manual."],[403.38,"But as it is, it doesn't, it can't really work on its own."],[409.24,"Okay. The only other real historical note I have by way of int introroduductionction. is, and this is getting back to that we talked about way back when we did that to the Torah"],[420.32,"And I talked about what's called the documentary hypothesis."],[423.12,"I talked about all that JEDP stuff."],[426.0,"This is a place that shows you one of the problems with that."],[429.76,"Okay. Because P, if you remember, and if you don't, I don't blame you at all."],[436.08,"P was the abbreviation for priest or priestly."],[439.62,"And we said, well, there's there's some truth in the idea that the people who were literate in ancient Israel were the priests."],[447.38,"So any editing work that was done, translation, updating of place names that happened to the text would have been done probably by priests, because they were literate, and they were the ones who had custody of the texts, to copy them and to and to do these things."],[465.35,"But so you will generally see, though, for people who hold to that, the idea that the book of Leviticus is this mishmash that was put together after the exile."],[480.87,"So the book of Leviticus, they will say, out of the Torah, did not exist before the Babylonian exile."],[488.39,"So it didn't exist in David's time, definitely didn't for them exist in Moses' time."],[493.45,"Didn't even exist in Elijah's time."],[495.77,"That's a good question."],[498.09,"So the assumption is, the assumption they make, as 19th century Germans are wont to do, is that everything evolves from simple to more complex."],[514.61,"And so as you read the book of Leviticus, especially when you get into some of the later material that's talking about the feasts, there's this very developed cycle of feasts and different observances and sacrificial rituals at those feasts and those kind of things."],[531.73,"Those are complex."],[534.26,"Therefore, they can't be early."],[537.22,"So this is a weirdly, yeah."],[539.22,"I mean, you could just anything that looks too complex to you, you can just label as late."],[543.19,"Okay. But that's an assumption."],[545.19,"'s an assumption. It"],[548.81,"It's also an assumption that requires you to take Israel and pretend that Israel and the Israelites are not part of the ancient Near East and ancient Near Eastern cultures."],[562.73,"Because by the time of Moses, the Babylonians and the Egyptians have a very complex cycle of feasts and religious observances."],[571.37,"Way more complex than what's here."],[575.89,"The Canaanites do"],[578.17,"Baal worship is already way more complicated than what's here."],[584.75,"And in different places."],[586.91,"Okay. So they've taken this, they've taken basically what they've done is they've taken the Protestant idea that Christianity is was a new religion."],[600.09,"So Jesus comes and the apostles just start from scratch with and then everything complex that develops out of that."],[608.09,"And they've shot that back into Judaism."],[612.41,"That well, Judaism is this new religion that starts around some Moses-ish figure, because most of them don't believe Moses ever existed."],[621.05,"But somewhere in the murky depths of the end of the Bronze Age, Judaism as a religion gets started."],[627.58,"And since it's a new religion, then it just right, doesn't it starts out simple."],[635.96,"So anything complex comes later."],[638.28,"But here's what's weird."],[639.64,"So they'll say that when they're talking about the Bible."],[643.88,"But if you set the Bible aside and ask these same Old Testament ancient Uri scholars where Israelite religion came from, they will say that it evolved from Canaanite paganism."],[658.04,"And they will say that even though there's zero evidence for this, literally zero, they will say that Yahweh was part of the pantheon with Baal."],[667.64,"And the Israelites kind of broke him off and did their old thing."],[671.32,"But then it evolved out of the already existing religion."],[675.37,"Well, you can't have it both ways."],[678.53,"If it evolved out of the existing religion, wouldn't it have all the complexity of the existing religion already?"],[686.13,"And then actually immediately start to become more complex because it's an evolution of that."],[693.35,"How can you do start from scratch and evolve from at the same time?"],[698.31,"It doesn't make sense."],[702.81,"That's why they talk about these two different hypotheses in two different contexts."],[708.01,"Because if you tried to put them together in the same context, it'd be pretty apparent that they contradict each other."],[714.57,"Okay But here's point two about why they can't be that late."],[718.49,"Even if you want to say it's late."],[720.9,"So there's you get this debate between the modernists and the fundamentalists in the early 20th century, and they both have a bias."],[731.54,"So the modernist wants to say everything in the Bible is as late as they can possibly make it."],[737.67,"So everybody was saying that St."],[740.39,"John's Gospel was written like in 150 AD for about a century."],[746.79,"And then a copy was found that dated to AD 120."],[750.63,"I mean it's a fragment, but it's still St."],[753.19,"John's Gospel. So now they're like, well, okay, it was written in the 90s."],[758.39,"But they're still gonna everything's as late as they can possibly make it."],[761.91,"Okay. But the fundamental, or let's not call them fundamentals because that's a loaded word now."],[767.87,"Like conservative evangelical scholarship has a coordinating bias the other way where they want to say everything is as early as they can possibly say it."],[777.16,"So there are there are people in that camp who take this reactionary position that like everything in the New Testament was written right before AD 70."],[788.36,"Everything. The book of Revelation, everything was written before AD 70."],[793.18,"So they just sort of go in the opposite extreme rather than kind of looking at what are our clues,? right"],[799.18,"Right. And with the Torah."],[800.96,"So you've got the one group chopping it up and trying to make every piece as late as they could possibly make it."],[807.04,"And then you've got the other group saying, no, it's all one hole, and Moses wrote it all by hand personally."],[813.78,"Including Moses writing that Moses was the most humble man to ever live, including Moses writing about his own death, burial."],[823.75,"So that's just the opposite extreme."],[825.76,"It's just being reactionary."],[829.34,"And so, as you see, we've been kind of doing approaches like, well, no, Moses wrote something."],[836.04,"We're told we know Moses wrote something."],[838.44,"What Moses wrote is the basis of what we still have today."],[842.22,"But even the Hebrew that we have today looks nothing like any language that existed when Moses was alive."],[848.72,"So it's not a one- toto- oneone."],[850.56,"This has been translated, this has been updated"],[853.48,"There are places, as we pointed out, where it'll say, like, this city, which used to be called this other name."],[859.96,"And the which used to be is what it was called in Moses' time."],[864.13,"Meaning someone has come in and updated it."],[866.63,"Later. That's not changing the text."],[869.93,"That's still says the same thing."],[873.37,"So that's all going on."],[875.69,"Okay. But so let's look at the idea of Leviticus being post exilic."],[881.19,"Or any part of it being post exilic."],[885.51,"What would you expect to see in a positive sense?"],[889.59,"Not if you were just trying to make everything as late as you could possibly make it."],[893.03,"But in a positive sense, what are some post-exilic things that might be reflected in a book like Leviticus?"],[899.55,"In a book that's giving instructions to priests about how to do sacrifices and how to how to worship God."],[907.17,"That after the exile, things start to shift and change, these practices, and none of those shifts and changes are do they try to read back, which is what you'd expect from them."],[917.49,"You'd expect the priest to be like, no, this is how we were always supposed to do it."],[921.73,"This isn't a change."],[923.09,"This isn't no, no, this is how we were all this is how God told us, told Moses to do it."],[928.13,"But you don't see that anywhere."],[929.81,"Now, maybe you could make a case."],[931.97,"Maybe you can make a case."],[934.01,"That there's some stuff here from before the exile."],[937.73,"What's's Israel Israel?"],[940.02,"And that this reflects how they were doing things at the temple in Israel."],[945.94,"Here's the problem, and we'll talk about this more when we're going through Deuteronomy."],[950.74,"Deuteronomy describes some of the same things that are described here in Leviticus, some of the same rituals and some of the same feasts, and describes them differently."],[960.1,"And the way Deuteronomy describes them is the way they were done once they had the temple."],[964.82,"So if this is being written late, so late."],[968.92,"Why doesn't it look more like Deuteronomy does?"],[972.12,"Why doesn't it match what they actually did later?"],[975.4,"So this makes sense in the context of tabernacle worship."],[982.24,"Now, tabernacle worship doesn't immediately cease."],[986.21,"You've got, depending on your dating, at least 250 years, possibly as many, as much as 500 years of tabernacle worship before the temple's built."],[998.53,"Somewhere between 250 and 500 years."],[1001.33,"So if you really just want to make it as late as possible, you could say this was written in like 993 BC, 500 years after Moses, if you take an early date of Moses and you push right as far as you can."],[1017.35,"I don't know why you'd want to do that."],[1019.43,"But at any rate, it's very clear that this is reflecting tabernacle worship."],[1023.75,"Whereas Deuteronomy is is reflecting temple worship."],[1029.15,"And the same people will use what I just said about Deuteronomy to argue that Deuteronomy is late."],[1035.71,"Because again, we don't care if our arguments in different areas contradict each other."],[1042.11,"We just care about trying to prove everything as late as is as late as we can possibly make it."],[1047.15,"We just care about that overarching agenda."],[1049.39,"We don't care if our my arguments here contradict my other arguments over there."],[1054.35,"When we get to Deuteronomy, I'll explain why there's that shift."],[1058.53,"And hint, Deuteronomy is right before they go into the land."],[1063.49,"So the way they do everything is about to change."],[1065.97,"They're not gonna be living nomadically anymore."],[1068.81,"They're gonna be settled."],[1070.13,"That's gonna change how you do certain feasts."],[1070.77,"If you're all living in one big camp, you're gonna do a feast one way"],[1073.53,"This kind of thing."],[1077.05,"If people have to travel to a central location, that's gonna change how to do it."],[1081.06,"Logistically. But all that is to say, hopefully, and hopefully you followed and it wasn't too nerdy, that the case for this being really late that people want to make it doesn't make sense."],[1092.52,"I can't prove Moses wrote anything."],[1094.68,"I can't prove Moses existed."],[1096.6,"You can't prove things like that."],[1098.92,"It's not how history works."],[1100.52,"It proved things."],[1102.68,"But the idea that this is post-exilic is kind of preposterous."],[1107.94,"So this goes back somewhere into the mosaic time period pretty clearly."],[1114.1,"So if that suffices for introduction to Leviticus, we'll get into the excitement that is."],[1121.26,"There's a lot of good stuff here, but it's not, you know, narrative is a lot easier for us to read."],[1129.95,"Stories, we kind of vibe with stories."],[1133.23,"And so things being presented in a moat."],[1135.63,"Just like when we went through the Gospels, it was a lot easier for people to connect with than like St."],[1140.19,"Paul's epistles in a lot of cases."],[1142.67,"You start getting into rarefied error when he's talking about justification and sanctification."],[1146.77,"Whereas when you're reading a story about Jesus, you know, healing someone attached."],[1150.53,"right We we vibe with that a little more easily."],[1152.64,"We connect with it more easily."],[1154.16,"So this is a little harder to connect with a lot of it, but hopefully, you know, I'll help we'll extract."],[1157.6,"There's a lot of important and good stuff here"],[1163.32,"So, all that said, unless anybody has any leftover comments or questions or reminiscences about Carl Jung, we'll go ahead and get started in Leviticus chapter 1, verse 1."],[1177.56,"Now the Lord called to Moses and spoke to him from the tabernacle of testimonies, saying, Speak to the children of Israel and say to them, If a man among you should offer gifts to the Lord from the cattle, the oxen or the sheep, you shall offer your gifts."],[1193.4,"So I'll pause there."],[1194.76,"So as I mentioned, we're gonna see most of the book of Leviticus is gonna be God talking to Moses. telling him what he needs to pass on to the people of Israel"],[1207.04,"And as we talked about before, Moses has this position of authority to not just repeat it."],[1213.65,"But he's going to be repeating it and applying it."],[1217.99,"Saying, here's the specifics."],[1220.17,"And all those sorts of things."],[1221.93,"But it's also important to watch, and we'll see why later on."],[1226.01,"We're not going to see this right away in this early part of Leviticus, but later in Leviticus, it's going to be bit very important to notice who God says Moses needs to speak to."],[1238.17,"So what we just saw is going to be the usual."],[1240.93,"Speak to the children of Israel."],[1242.41,"It's actually actually says sons of Israel, but you know."],[1245.21,"He says, Here here speak to the children of Israel."],[1247.57,"That's what he's going to say mostly."],[1249.57,"But there are going to be a few times where he says something different."],[1253.41,"Where he adds some more groups."],[1255.81,"And it's going to be important to notice that."],[1258.53,"Because most of the commandments in Leviticus are going to be directed toward the Israelites, not to everyone more generally."],[1270.93,"I'll just go ahead and comment on this a little now."],[1273.41,"Comment more as we go."],[1274.7,"The Israelites are not called to stop the Philistines from eating bacon."],[1278.62,"They're told they're not supposed to eat bacon."],[1281.44,"They're not told that they need to go and stop the Philistine from wearing mixed fabrics."],[1287.92,"They're told they're not allowed to wear mixed fabrics."],[1290.48,"And it's not just that"],[1293.88,"But then there are going to be other things that the Philistines do need to stop doing."],[1297.42,"Like human sacrifice and."],[1299.18,"I mean, there are things that they need to stop doing."],[1301.42,"But the purpose of these commandments that are being given to Israel is that Israel is supposed to be different than all the other nations."],[1308.94,"So if God just gives them the same commandments that apply to everybody, they're not going to be different."],[1315.52,"He's describing their way of life, and their way of life is supposed to be different and more holy, and supposed to represent God and his holiness to the other nations."],[1327.84,"So the intent is that Israel would be a light to the other nations, in that the other nations would look at Israel, see how Israel lives differently than them, see how God is blessing them because they're living differently, and then want to become like Israel by coming and worshiping Israel's God."],[1349.08,"Not to become Israelites, but to themselves become like Israel by putting away their idols and worshiping Israel's God."],[1356.28,"So that because God is the God of all the nations, not just of Israel."],[1360.2,"But for that to function, Israel has to be different."],[1363.88,"And this is going to be one of the tension points all through the rest of the Old Testament."],[1371.88,"Is that God is going to keep coming to them and saying, you're living just like everybody else."],[1376.28,"You're living just like the Phoenicians are, you're living just like the Philistines are are. you're living just like the Moabites"],[1381.68,"What happened to being an example?"],[1383.44,"And eventually it'll get so bad that God will say, Well, if you're not going to be a good example, I'm going to use you as a bad example."],[1390.56,"I'm going to punish you for your wickedness and let all the other nations see that."],[1394.72,"So if you won't be a positive message, you'll be a warning."],[1398.08,"It'll be a cautionary tale."],[1399.6,"That's what the prophets are going to come and start saying."],[1401.94,"And they're going to say that to Israel before it happens because again, it's a warning to them first."],[1407.54,"Look, repent, get back on track."],[1410.1,"Because if you don't, this is going to be the option God is going to have left."],[1414.9,"Because God has attached himself to Israel now."],[1418.34,"He is now the God of Israel."],[1420.26,"God is not going to let his reputation in the world be dragged down by. their behavior"],[1427.95,"So if they're going to be that way, his only option at that point is to make them a warning and a cautionary tale."],[1436.83,"To reveal that no, that is not what God is about."],[1443.15,"So that's where it's going to go."],[1445.23,"But here we're on the positive side."],[1447.33,"Here is the way to live your life."],[1449.49,"Follow these commands that I'm not giving to these other people."],[1453.49,"This is what Israel is called to be different."],[1456.09,"It's called to be."],[1457.49,"Israel should be, should relate to the other nations the way the priests within Israel relate to the rest of Israel."],[1465.19,"That's the goal."],[1466.63,"So they're held to this higher sort of standard deliberately"],[1466.96,"And this is sort of how to do that."],[1473.12,"Deliberately. And so this is the beginning of the commandments directed to Israel."],[1478.48,"And so this is talking about when someone is going to bring an offering, bring a sacrifice to God."],[1485.36,"And that's why it says from their oxen or sheep."],[1488.96,"Because that's the primary sacrificial animals, are going to be cattle and sheep, because these are the clean animals."],[1498.32,"That's already being presupposed, even though we haven't gotten into clean and unclean animals yet."],[1502.8,"Which we will in Levitic."],[1503.92,"That's in Leviticus 2."],[1505.76,"But these are clean animals."],[1508.4,"Most of the other nations of the world, especially once you get. to the Greeks, are sacrificing a lot of pigs"],[1517.16,"Frankly, because pigs breed fast and have big litters."],[1522.12,"So they're a good source of fast protein."],[1525.62,"And you have more around, you could sacrifice one and lose less."],[1529.95,"Whereas you sacrifice a sheep, or especially a cow, takes a lot longer to get another right bread, full sized cheaper cow."],[1541.31,"Okay. Verse 3."],[1543.55,"If his gift should be a whole burnt offering from the oxen, he shall offer a male without blemish."],[1550.03,"He shall offer it at the door of the tabernacle of testimony as acceptable before the Lord."],[1556.71,"Okay. So notice the without blemish."],[1562.95,"This is trying to get around the obvious, like, oh, I've got this one that's not looking so good."],[1568.15,"I've got this one that broke its leg."],[1570.07,"I've got this one that, whatever, the run to the litter."],[1573.35,"I'll give that one to God and keep all the healthy ones."],[1576.57,"So no. No."],[1579.58,"And the male, especially with oxen, the male is more valuable than the female in terms of breeding."],[1588.1,"If you've got a male who can breed, that male can reproduce with a whole bunch of females."],[1595.63,"When you're breeding them."],[1597.15,"Whereas a female.ale is limited in how many calves it can have in a way that the m isn't"],[1604.63,"So that's why it needs to be a male one."],[1606.79,"God's saying, I want the best and the most valuable one."],[1610.79,"That's the one you give to God, not the least valuable that you don't really need."],[1616.87,"Then he shall put his hand on the head of the burnt offering, and it will be accepted on his behalf to make atonement for him."],[1622.79,"Okay. So now notice who puts their hand on the head of the ox they're offering?"],[1629.91,"The guy who's bringing it, not the priest."],[1635.19,"That's not part of the sacrificial ritual."],[1637.75,"But that laying on of hands is a way of designating, saying, I'm giving this one to God."],[1643.11,"God. You indicate this one's for"],[1643.97,"Just like when someone's ordained, you lay hands on them."],[1648.67,"But it's the person who's bringing it."],[1650.27,"He brings it up to the tabernacle."],[1652.83,"He does that, and then he gives it to the priest to do the sacrifice."],[1657.71,"I know I've said this before."],[1659.63,"We don't understand the difference between worship now and worship then."],[1664.32,"So if we did worship now, the way they did worship then, what would happen is I would stand by the doors to the narthex on Sunday morning."],[1675.92,"Y'all would come up and hand me loaves of bread."],[1680.56,"I would disappear for a while while you all stood outside, and I would come later and give you you portions of the bread to take home with."],[1686.8,"You wouldn't come in"],[1693.88,"You wouldn't even come into the narthex."],[1695.88,"The narthex would be where all the Levites are doing their thing."],[1700.76,"And the nave of the church, where you come to worship, is the place only priests could go."],[1710.2,"And only really, except when they were picking things up to move them, as we're going to see later in Leviticus, in terms of actual worship taking place, that was just going to be the high priest going into the sanctuary once a year."],[1725.42,"So all of our worship now as Christians, because of what Christ did, has brought us all in."],[1736.04,"All the way up to where only priests could go."],[1739.08,"And that's men, women, children, all into that space."],[1743.56,"Every once in a while I have somebody come who's from a different background, and they're like, why don't you let anybody go back behind me?"],[1750.12,"Sanctuary to the altar."],[1751.9,"They just see that one distinction that's left."],[1755.58,"It sort of miss the eight distinctions that have collapsed."],[1760.75,"And there is still that one that hasn't collapsed because there is still holy and unholy."],[1766.83,"There is still a need for the altar for the place where God is to be kept holy and sacrosanct."],[1773.47,"And if everybody's just wandering through it, then that's not happening"],[1780.44,"And I've seen this in Protestant churches that have altars."],[1784.2,"There aren't that many of them."],[1785.56,"Talk about Lutherans, Anglicans."],[1787.65,"But I've seen where there's not worship going on, people just walk around them, set drinks on them."],[1794.34,"That's not keeping the space holy."],[1799.24,"That's not maintaining any kind of specialness, holiness, set-asideness, for that space."],[1807.42,"And that's why we still have that space."],[1811.12,"But everything else has been opened up in a way that it wasn't before."],[1815.2,"Like people talk about temple worship all the time"],[1815.56,"But so this is we're seeing worship."],[1819.64,"And you'll hear these people say, well, they use musical instruments and da da da."],[1823.16,"I'm like, you never would have seen any of that."],[1825.96,"You would have been standing outside the door waiting for your piece of cow to come back."],[1831.24,"That's not that's not anything anyone ever, saw, witnessed, took part in."],[1837.09,"Verse 5, he shall kill the young bull before the Lord, and the priests, Aaron's sons, shall offer the blood and sprinkle the blood all around on the altar by the doors of the tabernacle of testimony."],[1850.85,"Okay So the animal gets killed."],[1854.21,"How does it get killed?"],[1855.73,"How do they kill it?"],[1856.77,"Just as they kill it."],[1858.37,"So this is important."],[1859.57,"Return to And this is something we're going to turn on."],[1863.53,"Because as we go through Leviticus, one of the most important things, hopefully, we're going to get out of Leviticus, is an understanding of how sacrifices work."],[1870.01,"Because the way we think sacrifices work is mostly wrong."],[1873.69,"And right off the bat, most of us have probably sort of either been taught or absorbed that sacrificing something means killing it."],[1883.13,"No, it doesn't."],[1885.13,"And here's part of why the killing of the animal is not ritualized."],[1892.01,"There's no special knife."],[1894.05,"There's no special axe."],[1896.09,"I use those two examples because the Greeks and the Romans had sacrificial axes and sacrificial knives"],[1901.39,"They had special implements for doing it."],[1904.59,"There's none of that here."],[1905.71,"Remember, there are no commandments about making sacrificial knives out of bronze or anything else."],[1911.87,"You just kill it somehow."],[1914.99,"So there's no particular way of killing it that's mandated, that's part of the ritual at all."],[1921.07,"But then you immediately notice."],[1923.21,"Just kill it, however."],[1925.05,"But then there's very specific instructions about what to do with the blood."],[1928.57,"All the blood gets drained out of it."],[1932.54,"And that blood is taken and is put, poured out ultimately at the base of the altar."],[1940.54,"So why is that command so important?"],[1942.7,"Well, that command is so important because it's stopping them from doing a bunch of other things with the blood that the pagans used to do with the blood, like drinking it being the most important one."],[1956.5,"Eating and drinking blood."],[1958.34,"And we talked about this a little bit back when we talked about the flood in Genesis."],[1971.22,"Because after the flood, remember, Noah was given permission to eat animals for the first time, but was commanded not to eat or drink blood."],[1980.58,"Because the blood is the life of the animal."],[1983.44,"And we talked about how there's this progression in those early chapters of Genesis that in paradise, Adam and Eve are eating fruit."],[1991.12,"You can pick fruit all day, it doesn't hurt the tree."],[1995.2,"You're not harming anything, you're not killing anything."],[1999.44,"The tree is just offering you food."],[2002.41,"For you to pick, and then there will be more."],[2006.41,"At the expulsion from paradise, that moves to Adam needing to work the ground, needing to farm."],[2012.09,"Meaning sweat of his brow, thorns and thistles, clearing and breaking the ground, all that."],[2017.93,"Plus, then when it grows, you're ripping it up, you're killing it."],[2022.17,"And you're having to replant."],[2024.51,"So that's kind of a step down."],[2027.44,"So now God is giving humans permission to eat animals, at least some of them, in some circumstances."],[2034.77,"But the danger is God does isn't saying that humans can turn predatory."],[2046.19,"Humans are not predators."],[2048.83,"Or shouldn't be."],[2076.11,"This isn't just a quaint story, you know, or like any which way but loose."],[2080.47,"Oh, he's got an orangutan."],[2081.83,"That's hysterical. It's the idea is they're not afraid."],[2087.44,"There's this harmony restored."],[2089.52,"Because when that bear came around St."],[2091.2,"Seraphim, it wasn't afraid of him."],[2095.28,"And so it didn't need to protect itself to get defensive, to be violent with him."],[2099.62,"And they could live at peace together."],[2101.06,"Or Saint Jerome and his pet lion and on down the on down the line."],[2105.46,"But so the opposite of that is man becoming predatory."],[2108.74,"And so s,ort of the symbol of man becoming predatory is eating and drinking the blood."],[2116.58,"Like feeding on the life of the thing that you killed."],[2121.56,"And so God says, no."],[2123.92,"And so draining the blood, and doing these things to prepare the meat when you're going to eat a meat, eat the meat from an animal you've killed, is a process of valuing the animal and the life of the animal."],[2139.3,"And it conditions, how you approach the act of taking the life, the life of an animal and eating it."],[2147.76,"So if you've hunted and killed something and you eat it, that's very different than getting something from a factory farm."],[2156.65,"That's a very different phenomenon emotionally, spiritually, in every way."],[2162.75,"Or even if you have a farm yourself."],[2164.93,"And I remember when I was a kid, I watched my grandpa kill chickens, I watched my grandpa kill rabbits, I watched him grab a rabbit, cute bunny that we had been petting earlier in the day as kids."],[2176.51,"Grab it by the ears and slit its throat, hang it up and drain the blood out of it."],[2181.01,"That gives you a very different perspective on what's happening when you eat meat than if you just get something prepackaged."],[2187.25,"It's so different, in fact, that there's a classic of the internet where a woman really, really posted, you know, hunting is so horrible."],[2196.05,"Why don't you just get your meat at the grocery store like the rest of us, so no animals are harmed?"],[2201.26,"L,ike that's how different the experience that we can fool ourselves into thinking that."],[2207.15,"But so that's that's real."],[2209.23,"And the drinking of blood, the consuming of blood of the life of animals and of frankly, people"],[2216.63,"Human sacrifice almost always involved cannibalism."],[2219.99,"And the most common form of that cannibalism was drinking the blood of the person they sacrificed."],[2226.28,"This is right, a society, a culture."],[2229.66,"A ritual where humans are becoming predatory."],[2233.34,"Where they're they're literally going out, going to war, capturing prisoners, sacrificing them to their gods and drinking their blood."],[2241.98,"You don't get more predatory than that."],[2244.88,"Among humans. So God is cutting that off in terms of sacrificial ritual."],[2250.32,"It all gets poured out."],[2251.92,"It all goes to the altar, it all gets poured out."],[2254.36,"That life from the animal gets returned to the ground."],[2258.28,"With a couple exceptions."],[2259.56,"We saw one with the ordination."],[2260.84,"We're going to see that when they do the ordination, where a little bit gets rubbed on the priest."],[2265.08,"We've seen it get put on the altar to consecrate it."],[2269.08,"There's a few exceptions like that."],[2271.56,"But in no way is it ever ingested."],[2274.12,"That never happens."],[2275.8,"It gets poured out like this."],[2277.4,"And the fact that it's poured out at the base of the altar might bring to mind, if you remember back when we were going through the book of Revelation back in the long ago time, some of you weren't here."],[2288.44,"But back in the long ago time."],[2292.48,"Where were the the souls of the martyrs who were crying out to God how long they're under the altar in heaven, right where their blood was poured out because they offered themselves as a sacrifice to God."],[2307.2,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2314.56,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2319.92,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":25.04,"lv":2,"h":"New book: Leviticus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Leviticus</b> - \"everybody's favorite book of the Bible\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Worry each time through OT: <b>will people stop coming</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis, first half of Exodus = lots happening (Egypt, Sinai)</li><li class=\"d1\">second half of Exodus = <b>measurements, tabernacle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then Leviticus - \"tedious and boring\" - but you haven't quit</li><li class=\"d0\">Still <b>a lot of good stuff</b> in Leviticus</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro <b>shorter</b> than last ones - already did intro to the <b>Torah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":80.84,"lv":2,"h":"Setting: no new history","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus does <b>not narrate much history</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Exodus intro had Egypt, time period, background; <b>not here</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Structure</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chs 1-7</b>: God telling Moses things to tell Israel</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chs 11-27</b>: same - God telling Moses things to tell Israel</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>3 chapters in the middle</b> (8-10): <b>ordination of the priests</b> - only real narrative</li><li class=\"d0\">Picks up at <b>dedication of the tabernacle</b> (end of Exodus)</li><li class=\"d1\">ch 8 = ordination of priests; book moves forward \"<b>a few days</b>\" total</li><li class=\"d0\">Background of Leviticus = background of end of Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">same time, same place: <b>desert, just after leaving Sinai</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Numbers</b> picks up right on the other side</li></ul>"},{"t":212.87,"lv":3,"h":"Name of the book","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Name \"equally boring\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Leviticus</b> = Latin transliteration of Greek <i>Leviticon</i> = \"the Levite thing\" → <b>the book of the Levites</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"stuff for the Levites, for the priests\" - <b>the priest book</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew names of the five books = <b>first word</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis = <i>Bereshit</i> \"in the beginning\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Leviticus = <i>Vayikra</i> = \"<b>and he called out</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">1:1 \"Now the Lord called to Moses\" - Hebrew <b>verb-subject-object</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>vayikra</i> + <i>Yahweh</i> + Moses (with direct object marker)</li></ul>"},{"t":301.25,"lv":3,"h":"The continuity marker","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The <i>vav</i> in <i>vayikra</i> = marker of <b>continuous narrative</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew sentences begin with verbs - verb carries marker of <b>continuity</b> or <b>discontinuity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">good translations: \"<b>then</b>\" / \"<b>so</b>\" = continuity</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>but</b>\" / \"<b>afterward</b>\" / \"<b>later</b>\" = break</li><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus <b>begins with continuity marker</b> → first word ties back to <b>end of Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So Leviticus <b>can't function on its own</b></li><li class=\"d1\">strip out the 3 narrative chapters, keep \"God says to Moses, tell the Israelites\" → could be a <b>manual</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as it stands, it can't stand alone</li></ul>"},{"t":409.24,"lv":2,"h":"Documentary hypothesis problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>documentary hypothesis</b> (JEDP) from the Torah intro</li><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus shows <b>one of its problems</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>P = priestly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some truth: <b>literate</b> people in ancient Israel = priests</li><li class=\"d1\">editing, translating, updating place names → done by priests, who had <b>custody of the texts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Those who hold the theory say Leviticus is a <b>mishmash assembled after the exile</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't exist before <b>Babylonian exile</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not in David's time, <b>not in Moses' time</b>, not even Elijah's</li></ul>"},{"t":498.09,"lv":3,"h":"Simple-to-complex assumption","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Assumption of \"<b>19th century Germans</b>\": everything <b>evolves from simple to complex</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus has developed <b>cycle of feasts</b>, observances, sacrificial rituals → \"complex, therefore <b>can't be early</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"anything that looks too complex to you, you can just label as late\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Also requires pretending Israel <b>isn't part of the ancient Near East</b></li><li class=\"d1\">by Moses' time <b>Babylonians and Egyptians</b> have far more complex feast cycles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Canaanites</b>: Baal worship already way more complicated</li><li class=\"d0\">They took the <b>Protestant idea</b> that Christianity was a new religion starting from scratch → <b>shot it back into Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judaism = new religion around \"some Moses-ish figure\" (most don't think Moses existed) at end of Bronze Age</li><li class=\"d1\">new religion → starts simple</li></ul>"},{"t":638.28,"lv":3,"h":"Evolution vs starting from scratch","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same scholars, <b>setting the Bible aside</b>: Israelite religion <b>evolved from Canaanite paganism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"zero evidence\" - say <b>Yahweh was in the pantheon with Baal</b>; Israelites broke him off</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Can't have it both ways</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if evolved from existing religion → would <b>already have all the complexity</b>, then grow more</li><li class=\"d1\">\"how can you start from scratch and evolve from at the same time?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hence two hypotheses used in <b>two different contexts</b> - together they <b>contradict</b></li></ul>"},{"t":714.57,"lv":3,"h":"Dating bias, both directions","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Point two: why it can't be that late</li><li class=\"d0\">Early 20th c. debate - <b>modernists vs conservatives</b>, both biased</li><li class=\"d1\">modernist: everything <b>as late as possible</b></li><li class=\"d0\">John's Gospel said written <b>150 AD</b> for a century → fragment found dated <b>120 AD</b> → \"well, the 90s\" - still as late as possible</li><li class=\"d1\">conservative evangelical: everything <b>as early as possible</b></li><li class=\"d0\">reactionary: all NT, even <b>Revelation</b>, before <b>AD 70</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Torah same</li><li class=\"d1\">one group <b>chops it up</b>, every piece late</li><li class=\"d1\">other: \"<b>Moses wrote it all by hand</b>\" - incl. most humble man ever, and <b>his own death and burial</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"just being reactionary\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Our approach: <b>Moses wrote something</b> - it's the <b>basis</b> of what we have</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew today looks <b>nothing like Moses' language</b> → translated, updated</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this city, which used to be called…\" = <b>later updating</b>, not changing the text</li></ul>"},{"t":875.69,"lv":2,"h":"Post-exilic? Tabernacle vs temple","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Test: what would you <b>expect to see if post-exilic</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">After exile, <b>practices shift</b> - none of that read back</li><li class=\"d1\">you'd expect priests to say \"<b>this is how we were always supposed to do it</b>\" - not there</li><li class=\"d0\">Maybe some pre-exilic material reflecting temple practice? <b>Problem</b>: <b>Deuteronomy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">describes <b>same rituals and feasts, differently</b> - the way they were done <b>with the temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if Leviticus were late, why doesn't it <b>match Deuteronomy</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Leviticus makes sense in <b>tabernacle worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tabernacle period: <b>250-500 years</b> before the temple (depending on dating)</li><li class=\"d1\">latest possible: ~<b>993 BC</b> - \"don't know why you'd want that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same people use this to argue <b>Deuteronomy is late</b> - \"we don't care if our arguments contradict each other\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why the shift in Deuteronomy: <b>right before entering the land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no longer <b>nomadic</b> → feasts done differently; travel to <b>central location</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Conclusion: can't prove Moses wrote anything - \"not how history works\" - but <b>post-exilic is \"preposterous\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goes back to the <b>Mosaic period</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1177.56,"lv":2,"h":"Leviticus 1:1-2 - God speaks to Moses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Lev 1:1-2</b> read: Lord called to Moses from the <b>tabernacle of testimony</b>; \"speak to the children of Israel… if a man among you offers gifts… from the cattle, oxen or sheep\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of Leviticus = <b>God to Moses, to pass on</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses has <b>authority</b> not just to repeat but to <b>apply</b> - \"here's the specifics\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Watch <b>who Moses is told to speak to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">usual: \"<b>children of Israel</b>\" (really sons of Israel)</li><li class=\"d1\">a few times <b>adds other groups</b> - matters later</li><li class=\"d0\">Most commandments directed to <b>Israelites</b>, not everyone</li></ul>"},{"t":1270.93,"lv":3,"h":"Commands for Israel, not the nations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Israelites <b>not told to stop Philistines eating bacon</b> - told <b>they</b> can't eat it</li><li class=\"d0\">Not told to stop Philistines wearing <b>mixed fabrics</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some things Philistines <b>do</b> need to stop: <b>human sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Purpose: Israel to be <b>different from all other nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same commands as everybody → not different</li><li class=\"d1\">describes <b>their way of life</b>: more holy, <b>representing God and his holiness</b> to the nations</li><li class=\"d0\">Israel = <b>light to the nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nations see Israel living differently, <b>God blessing them</b> → want to become <b>like Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not become Israelites - <b>put away idols, worship Israel's God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God of all nations</b>, not just Israel - but Israel has to be different</li></ul>"},{"t":1363.88,"lv":3,"h":"Tension: example or warning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tension point</b> through rest of OT</li><li class=\"d1\">God: \"you're living just like the <b>Phoenicians, Philistines, Moabites</b>… what happened to being an example?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Eventually: \"if you won't be a <b>good example</b>, I'll use you as a <b>bad example</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">punish wickedness, nations see it → <b>cautionary tale</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>prophets</b> say it <b>before it happens</b> - warning first: \"repent, get back on track\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God has <b>attached himself to Israel</b> - now \"the God of Israel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">won't let his <b>reputation</b> be dragged down by their behavior</li><li class=\"d1\">only option left: make them a <b>warning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Here we're on the <b>positive side</b>: \"here is the way to live\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel relates to nations as <b>priests within Israel relate to the rest of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>higher standard, deliberately</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1478.48,"lv":2,"h":"1:2 - Offerings from clean animals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beginning of commands to Israel - <b>bringing an offering</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"From oxen or sheep\" = primary sacrificial animals: <b>cattle and sheep</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>clean animals</b> - presupposed already; clean/unclean comes later in Leviticus</li><li class=\"d0\">Other nations, esp. <b>Greeks</b>, sacrifice lots of <b>pigs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pigs <b>breed fast, big litters</b> → fast protein, lose less per sacrifice</li><li class=\"d1\">cow takes much longer to replace</li></ul>"},{"t":1541.31,"lv":2,"h":"1:3 - Male without blemish","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:3</b> whole burnt offering from the oxen: <b>male without blemish</b>, at the <b>door of the tabernacle</b>, acceptable before the Lord</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Without blemish</b>: blocks \"this one's broken its leg… <b>runt of the litter</b>, give that to God, keep the healthy ones\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Male</b> = more valuable than female for breeding</li><li class=\"d1\">one male breeds with many females; female limited in calves</li><li class=\"d0\">God: \"I want <b>the best and the most valuable</b>\" - not the least valuable you don't need</li></ul>"},{"t":1616.87,"lv":2,"h":"1:4 - Hand on the head","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:4</b> hand on head of the burnt offering → accepted on his behalf to <b>make atonement</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Who lays hand on? <b>The man bringing it, not the priest</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not part of sacrificial ritual - it's <b>designating</b>: \"this one's for God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>laying hands in ordination</b></li><li class=\"d0\">He brings it up to the tabernacle, then <b>gives it to the priest</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1657.71,"lv":3,"h":"Worship then vs now","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"We don't understand the difference between worship <b>now and then</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Worship the old way today: I stand at <b>narthex doors</b>, you hand me <b>loaves</b>, I disappear, then bring portions back</li><li class=\"d1\">you wouldn't even enter the narthex = where <b>Levites</b> work</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nave</b> = place only priests could go - and only to move things</li><li class=\"d1\">actual worship: <b>high priest into the sanctuary once a year</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now all Christians - <b>men, women, children</b> - brought <b>all the way in</b>, because of <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Visitors ask \"why can't anyone go behind the <b>sanctuary</b> to the altar?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they see <b>one distinction left</b>, miss the <b>eight that collapsed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">still <b>holy and unholy</b> - altar kept <b>holy and sacrosanct</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Protestant altars (Lutherans, Anglicans): people <b>set drinks on them</b> → no holiness, no \"set-asideness\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"People talk about temple worship\" - <b>musical instruments</b> etc.: you'd have been <b>outside waiting for your piece of cow</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1837.09,"lv":2,"h":"1:5 - Killing and the blood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:5</b> kill the young bull before the Lord; priests, <b>Aaron's sons</b>, offer the <b>blood</b>, sprinkle all around the <b>altar</b> at the door</li><li class=\"d0\">Key goal of Leviticus: understand <b>how sacrifices work</b> - \"the way we think they work is <b>mostly wrong</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Assumption: <b>sacrificing = killing</b> - \"<b>No, it doesn't</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Killing <b>not ritualized</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greeks/Romans: <b>sacrificial axes and knives</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here <b>no special implement</b>; \"you just kill it somehow\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>very specific instructions for the blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>all drained</b>, poured out at the <b>base of the altar</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1940.54,"lv":3,"h":"Why: man is not a predator","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why so important? Stops what <b>pagans did with blood</b> - above all <b>drinking</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to the <b>flood</b> (Genesis)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Noah</b> allowed to eat animals - but <b>not eat or drink blood</b>: \"blood is the <b>life</b> of the animal\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Progression in early Genesis</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>paradise</b>: eat fruit - picking doesn't hurt the tree, nothing killed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>expulsion</b>: Adam must <b>farm</b> - sweat, thorns - ripping up plants</li><li class=\"d1\">now <b>permission to eat animals</b> - but not to turn <b>predatory</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Humans <b>are not predators</b> - or shouldn't be</li><li class=\"d0\">Restored <b>harmony</b>: <b>St. Seraphim's bear</b>, <b>St. Jerome and his lion</b> - not afraid, so no need to be violent</li><li class=\"d0\">Opposite = man <b>predatory</b>; symbol = <b>eating and drinking blood</b>, \"feeding on the life of the thing you killed\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2121.56,"lv":3,"h":"Valuing the animal; human sacrifice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God says no → <b>draining the blood</b>, preparing meat = <b>valuing the animal and its life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">conditions how you approach <b>taking life and eating</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hunting and eating vs <b>factory farm</b> - \"very different emotionally, spiritually\"</li><li class=\"d1\">his <b>grandpa killing chickens and rabbits</b> - petted the bunny that morning, <b>slit its throat, drained the blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">classic internet post: \"hunting is horrible, why not get meat at the <b>grocery store</b> so no animals are harmed?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Drinking blood of animals - and <b>people</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>human sacrifice</b> almost always involved <b>cannibalism</b>, mostly <b>drinking the blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">war, capture prisoners, sacrifice to gods, drink blood - \"<b>you don't get more predatory</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God cuts it off: <b>all poured out</b> at the altar → life <b>returned to the ground</b></li><li class=\"d1\">few exceptions: <b>ordination</b> (rubbed on priest, put on altar to consecrate)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>never ingested</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Poured out at <b>base of the altar</b> → <b>Revelation</b>: souls of the <b>martyrs under the altar</b> crying \"how long\" - blood poured out as <b>sacrifice to God</b></li></ul>"}]});
